PART 6: BEST PRACTICES

================================================================================

Best Practice 1: Destructure When It Improves Readability

Common approach:

export default function Book({
  title,
  author,
  price
}) {

  return (
    <div>

      <h2>{title}</h2>

      <p>{author}</p>

      <p>${price}</p>

    </div>
  );
}


Direct props access is also valid:

export default function Book(props) {

  return (
    <div>

      <h2>{props.title}</h2>

      <p>{props.author}</p>

      <p>${props.price}</p>

    </div>
  );
}

Comparison
Destructuring                  | Direct props Access
-------------------------------|----------------------------------
Less repetition                | Keeps source object explicit
Props visible in signature     | Useful when forwarding props
Very common in React           | Also valid React
Good with a few known props    | Can be convenient in some cases


Destructuring is a common convention, but it is not mandatory.

================================================================================

Best Practice 2: Use Default Values Where Appropriate

================================================================================

export default function Button({
  label = 'Click Me',
  onClick = () => {},
  disabled = false
}) {

  return (
    <button
      onClick={onClick}
      disabled={disabled}
    >
      {label}
    </button>
  );
}


This keeps default behavior close to the parameter declaration.

Default Destructuring vs ||

Destructuring default:

label = 'Click Me'


is used for undefined.

Using:

label || 'Click Me'


also falls back for other falsy values such as:

''
0
false


This difference matters in some components.

================================================================================

Best Practice 3: Group Related Props

================================================================================

export default function UserCard({

  // User information
  name,
  email,

  // Display options
  showEmail = true,
  showRole = true,

  // Event handlers
  onEdit,
  onDelete

}) {

  return (
    <div>

      <h3>{name}</h3>

      {showEmail && (
        <p>{email}</p>
      )}

      <button onClick={onEdit}>
        Edit
      </button>

      <button onClick={onDelete}>
        Delete
      </button>

    </div>
  );
}


Benefits:

Benefit
--------------------------------
Organized
Easy to scan
Clear responsibilities
Better documentation


showRole is included in the original example as a display option, although the sample JSX doesn't currently use it.

