PART 7: COMMON MISTAKES

================================================================================

Mistake 1: Incorrect Function Parameters

Wrong:

export default function Book(
  title,
  author,
  price
) {

  return (
    <h2>{title}</h2>
  );
}


React passes the component's Props as an object.

Correct:

export default function Book({
  title,
  author,
  price
}) {

  return (
    <h2>{title}</h2>
  );
}

Easy Memory
React gives:

props = {
  title,
  author,
  price
}

Therefore destructure:

{ title, author, price }


================================================================================

Mistake 2: Mixing Destructured and Non-Destructured Props Incorrectly

================================================================================

Avoid:

export default function Book(
  { title, author },
  props
) {

  return (
    <div>

      <h2>{title}</h2>

      <p>
        {props.author}
      </p>

    </div>
  );
}


A normal function component receives one Props object as its primary component input.

Use:

export default function Book({
  title,
  author,
  price
}) {

  return (
    <div>

      <h2>{title}</h2>

      <p>{author}</p>

      <p>${price}</p>

    </div>
  );
}


================================================================================

Mistake 3: Trying to Modify a Destructured Prop

================================================================================

Avoid:

export default function Book({
  title,
  author
}) {

  title = 'New Title';

  return (
    <h2>{title}</h2>
  );
}


A component should treat its Props as immutable/read-only inputs.

Use the received value:

export default function Book({
  title,
  author
}) {

  return (
    <h2>{title}</h2>
  );
}

Important Clarification

Destructuring itself creates local bindings, but it doesn't make mutating prop-derived data an acceptable React pattern.

Think:

PROPS
  ↓
INPUT
  ↓
READ
  ↓
DON'T MUTATE


================================================================================

Mistake 4: Destructuring Too Deeply

================================================================================

Harder to read:

export default function BookCard({
  book: {
    details: {
      info: {
        title,
        author
      }
    }
  }
}) {

  return (
    <h2>{title}</h2>
  );
}


Cleaner:

export default function BookCard({
  book
}) {

  const {
    title,
    author
  } = book.details.info;

  return (
    <h2>{title}</h2>
  );
}

Why?
Deep Parameter Destructuring | Local Destructuring
-----------------------------|-------------------------------
Harder to scan               | Easier to read
Long function signature      | Cleaner signature
More fragile looking         | Structure easier to follow


================================================================================

COMMON MISTAKES - QUICK TABLE

================================================================================

Mistake                 | Avoid                       | Better
------------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------------
Wrong parameters        | Book(title, author)         | Book({ title, author })
Repeated access         | props.title everywhere      | Destructure when useful
Modify prop input       | Mutate prop-derived data    | Treat props as read-only
Very deep destructuring | Huge nested signature       | Destructure inside function
Blind ...rest spread    | Forward everything blindly  | Forward valid intended props


